## Key Observations

**Null displacement effect is robust to time-window choice.** Across all three horizons, the pooled $\delta^D$ ranges from +0.0046 to +0.0059 with $p$-values between 0.043 and 0.086 — no window delivers a clearly significant result and the near-zero point estimate is stable. The one closure that consistently drives the pooled estimate (dept\_133) remains significant at $h = 1$ ($\delta^D = +0.040$, $p < 0.001$) and $h = 2$ ($\delta^D = +0.037$, $p < 0.001$), but this is an outlier: all other closures yield $p > 0.05$ at every horizon.

**$\beta$ is the dominant and most stable parameter.** The mean-reversion term ($\beta \approx -0.03$ to $-0.08$ per closure) is consistently significant across closures and horizons, confirming that displaced consumers show a sharp relative drop in purchase frequency after reopening driven by regression to the mean rather than habit disruption.

